Rejected because the brief never says what happens when the same-wave conflict check itself fails (it cannot run, or it breaks the integration checkout). Leg (e) sends those failures through the same refuse-row-plus-tick-override path built for a real detected conflict. In that path a tick means "publish anyway", but for a check failure the human needs "retry". Result: a "tree" row ticked while the checkout is still broken lets the bundle through unchecked (adversary reproduced two conflicting draft PRs). An "unrun" row's text includes the error cause, so the tick stops matching when the cause changes and the human loops. The fold, by contrast, stops the run on a broken tree, so the two paths have different policies for the same failure.
Next plan: specify the check-failure behaviour explicitly. Either stop the way the fold does, or refuse with no tick override so that only a fresh, successful check clears the row. Also say what a tick means on each row kind, and give rows a key that does not depend on the free-text cause. Add a leg where the fault is still there after the tick.
